virglrenderer: a program the GL gives a too small VirglBlock is refused, no abort

What: bind_virgl_block_loc() reads the GL's size of a linked program's VirglBlock
(the sysval uniform block every vrend shader declares) into a variable that now
starts at 0. When the GL reports less than vrend's struct sysval_uniform_block, or
the query fails and writes nothing, the program is not used: add_shader_program()
destroys it and handles it as a failed link (remembered per fragment shader, its
draws skipped, logged as "the host refused a shader (link)", one VirglBlock line);
a separable pipeline's rebind skips the draw and tries again at the next one. The
link and compile status variables start at GL_FALSE for the same reason: a status
query that fails reads as a failure, not as stack garbage.
Why: the size was read into an uninitialized variable and checked with assert(),
and the runtime builds virglrenderer with assertions on: QEMU aborted once at
desktop start ("Assertion failed: ((size_t) virgl_block_size >= sizeof(struct
sysval_uniform_block)), function bind_virgl_block_loc"), right after the Mac's GL
had refused calls of the same context (quickshell: a valid shader failed to
compile with an empty log, fence syncs, buffer maps and framebuffer checks failed,
while macOS reconfigured its displays). The whole VM died (#547).
Upstream: the same assert is in upstream virglrenderer; not sent.
Test: Tests/virgl/test-sysval-block.c with sysval-block-gl.c (the GL's answer for
VirglBlock made 16 bytes, then a failed query: no abort, no draw reaches the GL,
one refused-link line; then new shaders draw, the refused program is not linked
again).

